1. What are Multiple Case Labels?
In a switch statement, when multiple values need to execute the same block of statements, we can use multiple case labels.
This is called:
Multiple Case Labels with the Same Statement

Basic Syntax
switch (expression) {

    case value1:
    case value2:
    case value3:
        // Common statements
        break;

    default:
        // Default statement
}

Here, value1, value2, and value3 execute the same statement block.
*****************************************************************************
Example:
//Hidden secrets-Switch case part 1
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Multiple_case
{

	public static void main(String[] args) 
	{
	   //aeiou->  a,A 



char ip;
Scanner sd=new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.println("enter the character");
ip=sd.next().charAt(0);
switch(ip)
{
  case 'A':
  case 'a':
	  System.out.println("A or a is a vowel");
	  break;
  case 'E':
  case 'e':
	   System.out.println("E or e is a vowel");
	   break;
  default:
	  System.out.println("Consonant");
	  
}
	    

	}

}
